<div class="wrap-narrow">
  <header class="page-head">
    <span class="eyebrow">Limits</span>
    <h1>What eui-score does not engrave</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Anything outside the subset is refused, with the character where the engraver stopped. The messages below
      are not copied in: each source is handed to the engraver when this page is shown, and its answer is what
      gets printed.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="prose">
    <% for refusal in refusals %>
      <div class="refusal">
        <h3><%= refusal["what"] %></h3>
        <pre><code><%- score_highlight(refusal["src"]) %></code></pre>
        <% if refusal["message"].nil? %>
          <p class="muted">Accepted by this version of the engraver.</p>
        <% else %>
          <p class="score-error"><%= refusal["message"] %></p>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <h2>No longer limits</h2>
    <p>
      Since EUI 0.11 (Soli 2.28.2), the engraver reads LilyPond as people write it: variables and
      <code>\global</code>, <code>\new Voice</code> with <code>\voiceOne</code>/<code>\voiceTwo</code>,
      <code>\new Lyrics \lyricsto</code>, <code>\language</code>, <code>\fixed</code>, <code>\transpose</code>,
      <code>q</code>, <code>\partial</code>, <code>\cadenzaOn</code>, spacers, <code>\repeat unfold</code> and
      <code>percent</code>, grace notes, the tenor, soprano, baritone and <code>treble_8</code> clefs, fingerings,
      <code>\markup</code>, <code>\mark</code>, <code>\ottava</code>, phrasing slurs, <code>\trill</code> and other
      ornaments, <code>\arpeggio</code>, <code>\glissando</code>, tremolos, and <code>\new ChordNames</code>,
      <code>\new DrumStaff</code> and <code>\new TabStaff</code> — each drawn as LilyPond 2.25 draws it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Since Soli 2.28.4, the server engraves with the same engraver as the browser: 64th and 128th notes, a
      tuplet inside a tuplet, up to four voices on a staff, several <code>\addlyrics</code> verses, any metre;
      a note longer than what is left of its bar is cut at the bar line into tied notes, as LilyPond's
      <code>Completion_heads_engraver</code> cuts it; the sustain pedal (<code>\sustainOn</code>,
      <code>\sustainOff</code>) is drawn and held; and LilyPond 2.24's jumps, <code>\jump "D.S. al Coda"</code>
      and <code>\fine</code>, are set against their bar line. The library, the shared pages and the PDFs show
      what the editor shows.
    </p>
    <p>
      Since Soli 2.28.5, real LilyPond files open. The layout settings nearly every file writes —
      <code>\override</code>, <code>\set</code>, <code>\tweak</code>, a <code>\with { … }</code> or a
      <code>\layout</code> block, header fields no title prints — are read and passed over, each named in a
      warning, so the music after them is engraved; <code>\stemUp</code>/<code>\stemDown</code> and
      <code>\autoBeamOff</code> are honoured. <code>\breathe</code>, <code>\caesura</code>, <code>\upbow</code>,
      <code>\downbow</code>, <code>\flageolet</code>, <code>\espressivo</code>, <code>\laissezVibrer</code>,
      <code>\repeatTie</code>, <code>\cresc</code> and <code>\dim</code> in words and text spans are drawn, and
      so are forced and cautionary accidentals (<code>cis!</code>, <code>cis?</code>). Several
      <code>\score</code> blocks, or a <code>\book</code> of <code>\bookpart</code>s, become movements, each
      starting on a line of its own. A clef or a key may change in the middle of a bar, and
      <code>\compressMMRests</code> gathers bars of rest into one.
    </p>
    <p>
      Since Soli 2.28.9, the piano's two staves are crossed as LilyPond crosses them: a beam over notes of both
      staves is drawn once between them, slurs and ties go from one staff to the other, <code>\autoChange</code>
      shares one voice between the hands, and <code>\showStaffSwitch</code> and <code>\crossStaff</code> are drawn.
      The common <code>\override</code>, <code>\set</code>, <code>\tweak</code>, <code>\omit</code> and
      <code>\hide</code> settings are followed instead of passed over (directions, offsets, sizes, beam grouping,
      mark and time-signature styles, note-head styles, bar numbers). <code>\RemoveEmptyStaves</code> hides a
      staff that rests for a whole system, and <code>\partCombine</code> sets two parts on one staff with
      <em>a2</em>, <em>Solo</em> and two voices. Lines are broken over the whole piece, and the
      <code>\paper</code> settings for widths, indents, ragged lines and pages, the space between systems and the
      number of systems or pages are honoured.
    </p>
    <p>
      The score editor now edits what the engraver draws in such files instead of leaving them to the text:
      staves changing key or metre apart, a tempo, a mark, a bar line or a repeat sign inside a bar,
      <code>\cadenzaOn</code>, a tuplet inside a tuplet, every clef the engraver draws, <code>\open</code> and
      <code>\stopped</code>, <code>\tiny</code> and <code>\small</code>, <code>\new TabStaff</code>,
      <code>\new CueVoice</code> and <code>\cueDuring</code>, and an ossia. <code>\override</code>,
      <code>\set</code>, <code>\tweak</code>, a staff's <code>\with</code>, <code>\layout</code> and
      <code>\paper</code> are kept where they are written.
    </p>
    <p>
      The score editor now also edits a staff made by <code>\partCombine</code> on the score, its two parts as two
      voices (the variables it names kept), and a piano staff made by <code>\autoChange</code> as one voice,
      used directly or inside a variable; a <code>\tweak</code> on an articulation, words or a dynamic stays
      with it. Still kept as written, its notes edited in the text: <code>\partCombineUp</code> and
      <code>\partCombineDown</code>, voices inside a part, and other music beside <code>\partCombine</code> or
      <code>\autoChange</code> on its staff.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two staves are condensed on one from the editor (Format ▸ Condense with the staff below), and split again;
      a part that says how the parts combine (<code>\partCombineApart</code>, <code>\partCombineSoloI</code> and
      the like) is edited on the score too. The editor writes the words printed over them in its language
      (<code>soloText</code>, <code>soloIIText</code>, <code>aDueText</code>): <em>1.</em>, <em>2.</em> and
      <em>a2</em>, or <em>à 2</em> in French, and the engraver prints them; a staff holds two parts, not three.
    </p>
    <p>
      Since its second round of LilyPond work, the engraver also draws smaller staves
      (<code>\magnifyStaff</code>, <code>StaffSymbol.staff-space</code> with <code>fontSize</code>, ossia and cue
      staves), a <code>\time</code> in the middle of a bar, a <code>\partial</code> later in a piece, a
      <code>\time</code> written in one staff only, polymetre with bars of one length
      (<code>\enablePolymeter</code>, <code>\scaleDurations</code>, <code>timeSignatureFraction</code>) and
      voices inside a voice. The accidentals of a note moved to the other staff are counted on that staff.
      Pages are spaced with LilyPond's springs (a crowded page squeezes, a page with room stretches, ink never
      collides), and beams are sloped and placed as LilyPond's quanting places them.
    </p>
    <p>
      The score editor also opens on the score what the engraver now reads of the metre: a <code>\time</code>
      in the middle of a bar, a <code>\partial</code> after the first bar (at a bar line or inside one), a
      <code>\time</code> written in one staff only, a metre drawn beside another with bars as long
      (<code>\set Staff.timeSignatureFraction</code> and <code>\scaleDurations</code>, or
      <code>\enablePolymeter</code>), voices inside a voice, and staves drawn smaller
      (<code>\magnifyStaff</code>, a <code>staff-space</code> and <code>fontSize</code> in a staff's
      <code>\with</code>). Each is written back where it was while the bars still say the same; a bar whose
      <code>\time</code> or <code>\partial</code> an edit has moved is written as a <code>\partial</code> of its
      length.
    </p>
    <p>
      Since its third round of LilyPond work — in the editor and the playground, and since Soli 2.40.2 on the
      library's pages, the shared pages and in PDFs — the engraver sets a chord's accidentals in a zig-zag, draws ledger
      lines only under the heads that need them, takes a beam past what stands under it, and shapes slurs as
      LilyPond scores them. <code>\shape</code> moves the control points of a slur, a phrasing slur or a tie;
      <code>extra-offset</code>, <code>Hairpin.shorten-pair</code> and <code>Hairpin.height</code> move and
      resize hairpins and curves. The <code>\paper</code> spacings around titles and markups
      (<code>top-markup-spacing</code>, <code>score-markup-spacing</code>, <code>markup-markup-spacing</code>)
      are laid out. An instrument may change in the middle of a staff (<code>instrumentName</code>,
      <code>midiInstrument</code>), <code>\transposition</code> is played — a clarinet in B flat sounds a tone
      under what is written — and <code>\midi { \tempo … }</code> sets the playback speed. Staves of different
      lengths are drawn, the shorter silent to the end; a tablature has the strings its
      <code>stringTunings</code> names (a bass's four), and <code>\new RhythmicStaff</code> is one line.
      <code>\partCombine</code> prints the words its context sets. New signs are drawn and played: the other
      ornaments (<code>\reverseturn</code>, <code>\prallprall</code>…) and fermatas
      (<code>\shortfermata</code>, <code>\verylongfermata</code>…), arpeggio arrows, brackets and parentheses,
      two-note tremolos, the sostenuto and soft pedals, and lyric elisions.
    </p>
    <p>
      Since Soli 2.40.2, a tablature draws the details LilyPond draws: grace notes' frets, dead notes
      (<code>\deadNote</code>, <code>\xNote</code>) as crosses, a harmonic's fret between angle brackets and a
      tied note's fret in parentheses where a line starts with it, on up to eight strings; fret boards and
      diagrams of a ukulele's four strings. Shape notes are engraved (<code>\aikenHeads</code>,
      <code>\sacredHarpHeads</code>, <code>\southernHarmonyHeads</code>, <code>\funkHeads</code>,
      <code>\walkerHeads</code>, <code>shapeNoteStyles</code>), and so is Roman numeral analysis under the bass
      (<code>\markup \rN { V 6 5 }</code> in a <code>Lyrics</code> context). Words outside the scores — a
      <code>\markup</code> before, between or after them — are set as blocks of the page, laid out with
      <code>\column</code>, <code>\fill-line</code>, <code>\wordwrap</code> and the other markup commands; a page
      of words before a <code>\pageBreak</code>, or in a <code>\bookpart</code> of its own, is a title page. Each
      <code>\bookpart</code>'s own <code>\paper</code> sets its margins, headers, footers and page numbers, and
      page headers read LilyPond 2.24's <code>\if</code> and <code>\unless</code>.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Metre</strong>: staves whose bars last differently (polymetre with bars of different lengths)
        are refused: line them up with <code>\scaleDurations</code> or <code>timeSignatureFraction</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Editor</strong>: the notes of a staff scaled by <code>\scaleDurations</code> keep their
        lengths on the score (their pitches, marks and rests are edited; note input, lengths, pasting and a new
        metre there are refused, edited in the text), a drawn metre that changes after a staff's start
        (<code>timeSignatureFraction</code>) is left to the text, and staves whose bars last differently stay
        the engraver's alone, and so are a tablature of other strings than a guitar's six and a
        <code>\new RhythmicStaff</code> (their notes edited in the text).</li>
      <li><strong>Settings</strong>: an <code>\override</code> not in the lists above is still passed over
        with a warning.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="callout">
      Not sure? Paste your source into the <a href="<%= playground_path() %>">playground</a>: the engraver's
      message points at the exact character where it stops.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
